    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-3.1.04-01",
      "block_type": "definition",
      "heading": "Why population growth differs",
      "text": "Population growth differs because fertility, mortality and migration vary between places and periods. Social, economic, environmental and policy conditions influence these components, while age structure affects the observed rates. A good explanation identifies the component first, then follows a relevant mechanism. It should not assume that every low-income or high-income country has an identical demographic pattern.",
