      "block_id": "BL-6137-3.1.04-02",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "Social influences on births include education, marriage and partnership patterns, gender roles, knowledge and access to voluntary reproductive-health services. Longer education and later childbearing can contribute to fewer births during a particular period, depending on people's choices and circumstances. The mechanism involves opportunity, timing and control rather than a claim that educated people all choose the same family size.",
