    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-3.1.04-05",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "Social security and reliable livelihoods can alter dependence on children for later support. Better child survival can also change family decisions over time, although the response is not immediate or uniform. A falling infant mortality rate need not produce an identical birth-rate fall in the same year. Demographic change can involve delays and interactions, which is why a one-year comparison does not establish a complete causal transition.",
