    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-3.1.04-07",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "China is the best-known example of population control. From about 1979-80 to 2015 its one-child policy limited most couples to one child; couples who had extra children could be fined, although rural couples whose first child was a girl could often have a second. Births fell, but the policy contributed to a male-biased sex ratio and rapid ageing, leaving fewer young workers to support more older people. All couples were allowed two children from 1 January 2016, and on 31 May 2021 China announced that couples could have a third child, with support measures set out in July 2021 to encourage births.",
      "claim_ids": [
        "CL-6137-3.1.04-1",
        "CL-6137-3.1.04-2",
        "CL-6137-3.1.04-01-1",
