      "block_id": "BL-6137-3.1.04-11",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "Social and environmental influences on deaths include living conditions, food security, conflict, unsafe water, pollution and hazards. Their effects depend on exposure and response capacity. A flood can damage sanitation or services, while a prolonged food-access problem can weaken health. Disease can also change death rates sharply. Namibia was one of the five countries worst affected by HIV/AIDS: about one adult in five was living with HIV in 2004, and life expectancy at birth had fallen to 49 years by 2002, as many young adults died. Antiretroviral treatment, expanded from the mid-2000s, lets people with HIV live much longer.",
      "claim_ids": [
        "CL-6137-3.1.04-1",
        "CL-6137-3.1.04-2",
        "CL-6137-3.1.04-01-1",
        "CL-6137-3.1.04-01-2",
        "CL-6137-3.1.04-02-1",
        "CL-6137-3.1.04-02-2",
        "CL-6137-3.1.04-03-1",
