    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-3.1.04-16",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "A time-series interpretation should separate rate and number. Birth rates may fall while the annual number of births stays substantial because many people are at childbearing ages. This population momentum is one reason a slower fertility pattern need not immediately stop growth. The age structure inherited from earlier births matters. A policy evaluation needs a suitable period to distinguish intention, early response and longer demographic effects.",
      "claim_ids": [
