    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-3.1.04-18",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "Comparisons should account for population composition. Two countries with the same fertility behaviour at each age can have different crude birth rates if one has a larger share at childbearing ages. Similarly, migration of young adults can change future births without a direct policy change. The crude indicator is useful but combines behaviour with structure. A more detailed explanation needs the relevant age pattern rather than assuming that one national rate reflects identical decisions by all residents.",
      "claim_ids": [
