    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-3.1.04-19",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "A mortality example illustrates the age issue without claiming actual country figures. A has a large older share and a crude death rate of twelve per 1,000; B is much younger and has ten. Those numbers alone do not prove worse healthcare at A. Older populations face more deaths because of their composition, while age-specific survival could still be better. Relevant life-table or age-specific evidence would be needed for the healthcare comparison.",
      "claim_ids": [
