    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-3.1.04-20",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "Birth policies also take time to affect a population. Changes in childcare or service access may influence decisions over several years, while children born under a new pattern enter working ages much later. A short-term employment problem cannot be solved immediately by a measure intended to support births. Linking the policy to its timescale helps distinguish present service support from future age-structure effects.",
      "claim_ids": [
